```{r helpers, include = FALSE}
# Formats a number to two significant figures, keeping trailing zeros.
fmt <- function(x, digits = 2) {
  sub("\\.$", "", formatC(signif(x, digits), digits = digits, format = "fg",
                           flag = "#"))
}
# Formats an estimate and its interval for the text and the tables.
ci <- function(v) {
  v <- fmt(unname(unlist(v))[1:3])
  paste0(v[1], " (", v[2], "--", v[3], ")")
}
# Formats the range of a set of values, or one value where they agree.
range_text <- function(x) {
  r <- unique(fmt(range(x)))
  paste(r, collapse = " to ")
}
# Formats names as code, singly or as a list for the text.
eq <- function(x) paste0("`", x, "`")
eqs <- function(x) {
  x <- eq(x)
  if (length(x) < 2) return(x)
  paste(paste(x[-length(x)], collapse = ", "), "and", x[length(x)])
}
# The model weights of a screened set, named by equation.
set_weights <- function(x) {
  stats::setNames(x$mod_stats[x$success_models, "wi"], x$success_models)
}
```

[e1]: https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html
[e2]: https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2.html
[e3]: https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html
[e9]: https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example9.html

# Overview

Concentration-response experiments are rarely a single series. Replicates are
often held in shared vessels (e.g. tanks, vials or chambers), and a test may be
repeated on several plates, in several batches, or for several chemicals.
Ignoring that structure treats observations as independent when they are not,
and may change both an estimate and its uncertainty.

`bayesnec` provides three routes for such data: a group-level term in the model
formula, a separate model set for each level fitted by `bnec_group()`, and a
joint refit of those separate fits by `bnec_joint()`. Which route applies
depends on the kind of grouping (see the table under *Types of grouping*), and
here we apply all three to the `lum31` bioluminescence data of @luter2025.

```{r libraries}
library(bayesnec)
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
```

Each plate of `lum31` spans more than three orders of magnitude of
concentration, so each figure is drawn on a log axis. `autoplot()` returns the
predictor on the scale it was recorded on, having inverted the `log()` written
inside `crf()`, so the axis is set by transforming the scale and not the values
or the breaks (see *The scale of the predictor* in [Single model usage][e1]).
`rlu_axes` names and formats the axes of the `autoplot()` figures.

```{r log-axis}
log_x <- scale_x_continuous(transform = "log10",
                            labels = function(x) signif(x, 2))
rlu_axes <- list(
  labs(x = "Dissolved metal (mg/L)", y = "Relative light units"),
  scale_y_continuous(labels = function(y) format(y, big.mark = ",",
                                                 scientific = FALSE))
)
```

# Types of grouping

The route for a grouping follows from two of its properties: whether each level
spans the concentration series, and whether the levels are a sample from a
larger set (e.g. plates, which could have been any plates) or treatments chosen
by the investigator (e.g. copper and zinc).

| Grouping | Spans the series | Levels | Route | In this vignette |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Replicate vessels at one concentration (e.g. tanks, chambers, or the wells at one concentration on a plate) | No | A sample of possible vessels | `ogl()` on the vessel | `ogl(conc_group)` |
| Repeated runs of the whole test (e.g. plates, batches, genotypes drawn from a population) | Yes | A sample of possible runs | `ogl()`, `(par \| group)` or `pgl()` on the run | `pgl(plate)` |
| Treatments of interest (e.g. chemicals, species, exposure times) | Yes | Fixed, chosen by the investigator | `bnec_group()`, a model set per level | `toxicant` |
| Treatments to be contrasted in one posterior, or sharing a group-level term | Yes | Fixed | `bnec_joint()` on a `bnec_group()` fit | copper and zinc with a shared `(ec50 \| batch)` |

A vessel at one concentration has no curve of its own, because it is observed at
a single concentration, so it admits only a displacement, and `ogl()` is the
term for it. A grouping whose levels each span the series can also differ in
shape. `(par | group)` places a deviation on one named parameter where there is
a hypothesis about which feature of the curve varies (for example,
`(nec | group)` for a threshold that differs between levels), and `pgl()` places
one on each parameter where there is no such hypothesis. Treatments of interest
are not a sample from a larger population, so shrinking them towards a common
curve would answer a different question, and their functional forms may also
differ. The routes can be combined (e.g. the copper and zinc fits below include
group-level terms within each level of a `bnec_group()` call). The figure below
shows what each term does to a curve.

```{r fig-grouping-schematic, echo = FALSE, fig.asp = 0.75, out.width = "85%", fig.cap = "The effect of each group-level term on a nec4param curve, drawn from chosen parameter values without fitting. The dashed black line is the population curve and each colour one level of the grouping. (a) ogl() scales each level's curve by one factor, with the shape shared. (b) (nec | group) shifts the threshold along the axis and shares the rest of the curve. (c) pgl() gives every parameter a deviation, so each curve differs a little in each respect. (d) For a grouping within a concentration, each level (grey point) is one mean at its own concentration, displaced from the curve, with no curve of its own. On an ECx equation, (ec50 | group) acts as (nec | group) does in (b)."}
local({
  nec4 <- function(x, top = 1, bot = 0.1, beta = log(0.6), nec = 4) {
    bot + (top - bot) * exp(-exp(beta) * (x - nec) * (x > nec))
  }
  x <- seq(0, 12, length.out = 241)
  panel <- c("(a) ogl(group)", "(b) (nec | group)", "(c) pgl(group)",
             "(d) ogl() within a concentration")
  u <- c(-0.22, -0.07, 0.08, 0.2)
  curves <- rbind(
    data.frame(panel = panel[1], level = rep(letters[1:4], each = length(x)),
               x = x, y = nec4(x) * exp(rep(u, each = length(x)))),
    data.frame(panel = panel[2], level = rep(letters[1:4], each = length(x)),
               x = x, y = nec4(x, nec = 4 + rep(5 * u, each = length(x)))),
    data.frame(panel = panel[3], level = rep(letters[1:4], each = length(x)),
               x = x, y = nec4(x, top = exp(rep(u / 2, each = length(x))),
                               bot = 0.1 * exp(rep(rev(u), each = length(x))),
                               beta = log(0.6) + rep(c(0.3, -0.2, 0.1, -0.3), each = length(x)),
                               nec = 4 + rep(c(0.4, -0.6, -0.2, 0.5), each = length(x))))
  )
  population <- data.frame(panel = rep(panel, each = length(x)), x = x, y = nec4(x))
  vessels <- expand.grid(x = c(0.5, 2.5, 4.5, 6.5, 8.5, 10.5), rep = 1:3)
  vessels$y <- nec4(vessels$x) * exp(rep(c(0.12, -0.1, 0.05, -0.15, 0.18, -0.05,
                                           -0.08, 0.15, -0.12, 0.1, -0.2, 0.07,
                                           0.03, -0.04, 0.14, 0.06, -0.09, -0.16),
                                         length.out = nrow(vessels)))
  vessels$panel <- panel[4]
  ggplot(mapping = aes(x = x, y = y)) +
    geom_line(data = curves, aes(colour = level), linewidth = 0.6) +
    geom_line(data = population, linetype = 2, colour = "black", linewidth = 0.5) +
    geom_segment(data = vessels, aes(xend = x, yend = nec4(x)), colour = "grey60",
                 linewidth = 0.3) +
    geom_point(data = vessels, colour = "grey40", size = 1.6) +
    facet_wrap(~panel, ncol = 2) +
    scale_colour_hue(guide = "none") +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(axis.text = element_blank(), axis.ticks = element_blank()) +
    labs(x = "Concentration", y = "Response")
})
```

# Group-level term syntax

`ogl()`, `(par | group)` and `pgl()` are written into a `bayesnecformula`, and
`make_brmsformula()` (the function `bnec()` uses to build the non-linear `brms`
formula it fits) shows what each term becomes. We use `nec4param`, which
includes each parameter a term can be placed on (`top`, `bot`, `beta` and
`nec`). `make_brmsformula()` needs only the column names of the data, so we use
a small synthetic data frame with a proportion as the response and a `Beta`
family. The identity link is stated because a family object held in a variable
keeps its own link, and `Beta()` defaults to `logit`.

```{r syntax-data}
set.seed(228)
gdat <- data.frame(
  conc     = rep(c(0.1, 0.3, 1, 3, 10, 30), each = 4),
  response = rep(c(0.90, 0.88, 0.70, 0.40, 0.15, 0.05), each = 4) +
             rnorm(24, 0, 0.02),
  group    = factor(rep(letters[1:4], times = 6))
)
beta_fam <- Beta(link = "identity")

brms_form <- function(formula, family = beta_fam) {
  make_brmsformula(bayesnecformula(formula), data = gdat,
                   family = family)[["nec4param"]]
}
```

`ogl()` gives each level one deviation applied to the whole fitted mean, so the
curve is displaced and its shape is shared (the analogue of a random intercept
in a linear model).

```{r form-ogl}
brms_form(response ~ crf(conc, "nec4param") + ogl(group))
```

The equation appears intact as `bnecmu`, and the deviation `ogl` is applied
through `bnecmu * exp(ogl) / (1 - bnecmu + bnecmu * exp(ogl))`, which adds it to
the log-odds of the mean. How the deviation is applied depends on the family: it
is added to the mean under `gaussian()`, multiplies the mean by `exp(ogl)` under
`Gamma(link = "identity")`, and is added on the log-odds under `Beta`. Its
standard deviation (SD) is therefore read on that scale and not on the response
scale (e.g. under the `Gamma` family used for `lum31`, an SD of 0.1 corresponds
to a departure of about 10 per cent). The *Priors for group-level parameters*
section of the [Priors][e3] vignette lists which deviations are applied in this
way.

`(par | group)` places a deviation on one named parameter, and the other
parameters stay population-level (e.g. `(nec | group)` adds `+ (1 | group)` to
`nec` only). However, the parameters differ between equations (e.g. `bot` is a
parameter of `nec4param` but not of `nec3param`). Where the named parameter is
absent, the term is dropped from that equation, and an equation left with no
term on the grouping variable is excluded from the set, because it would
otherwise be fitted as one curve pooled over the levels that the other equations
separate. `bnec()` prints a message naming an excluded equation before fitting,
and `check_formula()` with `run_par_checks = TRUE` shows the dropped terms
without fitting anything:

```{r check-formula, message = TRUE}
invisible(check_formula(
  bayesnecformula(response ~ crf(conc, c("nec3param", "nec4param")) +
                    (bot | group)),
  data = gdat, run_par_checks = TRUE
))
```

The message names `nec3param` as the equation without a `bot` parameter, and
`(bot | group)` is the only term on `group` in this formula, so `bnec()` would
exclude `nec3param` and fit `nec4param` only.

`pgl()` places a deviation on each parameter of the equation, so each level has
its own curve, shrunk towards a common one, and the parameter names do not have
to be known in advance.

```{r form-pgl}
brms_form(response ~ crf(conc, "nec4param") + pgl(group))
```

`beta` and `nec` take their deviations additively, and `top` and `bot`, which
the `Beta` family bounds, take theirs through `topgl` and `botgl` in the
transformed form shown above for `ogl`. `pgl()` therefore estimates one SD per
parameter (four for `nec4param`) from however many levels the grouping has. Five
levels is the conventional minimum for estimating one SD, so `pgl()` is likely
to need more levels than the other terms to be well determined.

# The bioluminescence data

`lum31` contains acute copper and zinc toxicity tests on the tropical marine
bacterium *Vibrio azureus* (strain Lum-31), reported by @luter2025 at
<https://doi.org/10.1002/tox.70003>. Light output falls as the cells are
inhibited, so the response is the luminescence reading itself (in relative light
units). Copper and zinc were tested repeatedly as reference toxicants to show
that the assay is repeatable, which gives the data replication at four nested
scales (table below; see also `?lum31`).

```{r lum31-data}
data(lum31)
```

```{r lum31-design, echo = FALSE}
design_counts <- function(d) {
  c(n_distinct(d$well), n_distinct(d$conc_group), n_distinct(d$plate),
    n_distinct(d$batch))
}
design <- data.frame(
  scale = c("Well", "Cell", "Plate", "Batch"),
  column = c("`well`", "`conc_group`", "`plate`", "`batch`"),
  unit = c("One reading of one well",
           "The four wells at one concentration on one plate",
           "One complete test of 11 concentrations",
           "The plates dosed and read on one date"),
  one_plate = design_counts(subset(lum31, plate == "Zn 23Apr24 Rep6B" &
                                     minutes == 15)),
  arm = design_counts(subset(lum31, toxicant == "Zn" & minutes == 15))
)
knitr::kable(design, col.names = c("Scale", "Column", "Unit", "One plate",
                                   "Zinc at 15 minutes"))
```

Copper has 16 plates (176 cells and 704 wells) in the five batches, and each
well was read again at 30 minutes. We call each combination of toxicant and
exposure time an arm. Concentrations are measured dissolved metal, and each
batch was dosed separately, so a given dilution step is at a slightly different
concentration in each batch.

```{r fig-lum31-raw, fig.asp = 0.6, out.width = "95%"}
ggplot(lum31, aes(x = conc, y = rlu_cens, group = plate,
                  colour = toxicant)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.3, size = 1) +
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "line", linewidth = 0.3) +
  facet_wrap(~toxicant + minutes, scales = "free", labeller = label_both) +
  log_x +
  # The facet strip names the toxicant, so a legend would repeat it.
  scale_colour_hue(guide = "none") +
  theme_classic() +
  labs(x = "Dissolved metal (mg/L)", y = "Relative light units")
```

In each arm, luminescence stays near its control level over the lower part of
the series and then falls across about a tenfold range of concentration. Zinc is
less potent than copper by about an order of magnitude, and both metals are more
potent at 30 minutes. At the highest concentrations the readings reach the
instrument's floor. The plates of one arm differ by a factor of four to five at
the control, which is likely to reflect mainly the gain of the plate reader, set
separately for each read.

Dividing each plate's cell means by its mean at the lowest concentration divides
out that gain, so that the positions of the plates' curves can be compared.
`conc_rank` numbers the concentrations of a plate from lowest to highest (read
from the suffix of `conc_group`):

```{r lum31-15min}
zn_cu_15 <- droplevels(subset(lum31, minutes == 15)) |>
  mutate(conc_rank = factor(as.integer(sub(".*-c", "",
                                           as.character(conc_group)))))
```

```{r fig-plate-relative, fig.asp = 0.5, out.width = "95%"}
zn_cu_15 |>
  group_by(toxicant, batch, plate, conc_rank) |>
  summarise(conc = mean(conc), cell_mean = mean(rlu_cens),
            .groups = "drop_last") |>
  mutate(relative = cell_mean / cell_mean[conc_rank == "1"]) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = conc, y = relative, group = plate, colour = batch)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = 2, colour = "grey60") +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_point(size = 0.8) +
  facet_wrap(~toxicant, scales = "free_x", labeller = label_both) +
  log_x +
  theme_classic() +
  labs(x = "Dissolved metal (mg/L)",
       y = "Cell mean relative to the plate's\nlowest concentration",
       colour = "Batch")
```

For zinc, 12 or 13 of the 17 plates have a relative mean above 1 at each of the
second to fifth concentrations (medians of 1.01 to 1.03), so luminescence
appears to rise above the control before it falls (hormesis), whereas no copper
plate does. Once the gain is divided out, the plates of one metal still appear
to differ in where their curves fall. For example, the two 1Oct24 copper plates fall at
higher concentrations than every plate of the other batches, and the two 1Oct24
zinc plates fall above most of them.

We fit the raw luminescence and do not divide it by a control. Dividing by an
estimated control mean induces correlation between the observations that share
the divisor [@Ritz2026], whereas `ecx()` already measures an effect from the
fitted control (see *Preparing the response* in [Single model usage][e1]), and
the gain of each plate can be estimated by a plate term instead (see *Grouping
across the concentration series*). Readings at the floor are left-censored
through `rlu_cens | cens(censoring)`, and are not treated as zeros from a
separate process (see `?lum31`, and *Censoring* in [Single model usage][e1]). In
addition, a `Gamma` family with a constant shape has a constant coefficient of
variation, whereas the coefficient of variation within a cell rises as the
response falls (figure below), so each fit includes `disp("power")` (see
*Non-constant dispersion* in [Single model usage][e1], and *The dispersion
sub-model* in [A complete analysis workflow][e9]). The
single-plate and plate fits use the zinc readings at 15 minutes, and all fits
use a `Gamma` family with the identity link:

```{r lum31-zn15}
zn15 <- droplevels(subset(lum31, toxicant == "Zn" & minutes == 15))
gfam <- Gamma(link = "identity")
```

```{r fig-cell-cv, fig.asp = 0.6, out.width = "70%"}
cells <- zn15 |>
  group_by(conc_group) |>
  summarise(cell_mean = mean(rlu_cens),
            cv = sd(rlu_cens) / mean(rlu_cens), .groups = "drop") |>
  mutate(step = as.integer(sub(".*-c", "", as.character(conc_group))))
step_medians <- cells |>
  group_by(step) |>
  summarise(cell_mean = median(cell_mean), cv = median(cv))

ggplot(cells, aes(x = cell_mean, y = cv)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.4, size = 1) +
  geom_line(data = step_medians, linewidth = 0.5) +
  geom_point(data = step_medians, size = 1.8) +
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log10",
                     labels = function(x) format(x, big.mark = ",",
                                                 scientific = FALSE)) +
  theme_classic() +
  labs(x = "Cell mean (relative light units)",
       y = "Coefficient of variation within the cell")
```

Each grey point is one of the 187 cells of the zinc arm (four wells each), and
the black line joins the medians at each dilution step. The coefficient of
variation is about `r round(step_medians$cv[1], 2)` at the lowest concentration
and rises to about `r round(step_medians$cv[11], 2)` where the response is
lowest.

# Grouping within a concentration

The commonest structure in ecotoxicology is several replicate vessels at each
concentration (e.g. tanks or chambers). In `lum31` the vessel is the cell (one
level of `conc_group`). We place the term on `conc_group` and not on `well`:
within one exposure time each well has a single reading, so a term on `well`
would give each observation its own deviation, which cannot be separated from
the residual. To keep the plate structure out of this example we fit one plate
at 15 minutes, which has one cell at each of its 11 concentrations.

```{r one-plate}
one_plate <- droplevels(subset(lum31, plate == "Zn 23Apr24 Rep6B" &
                                 minutes == 15))
```

Both fits use `model = "all"`, which `bnec()` filters to the equations valid for
the family and predictor. We do not use `decline`, because it includes no
equation whose curve can rise above the control, and most zinc plates do (figure
of plate-relative means). No fit in this vignette names `iter`, `warmup` or
`control`, so each samples at `bnec()`'s defaults, and a diagnostic shown here
is what a reader would obtain from the same call.

```{r fit-conc-group, results = "hide"}
fit_plain <- bnec(rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), "all") +
                    disp("power"),
                  data = one_plate, family = gfam,
                  seed = 228, refresh = 0)

fit_ogl <- bnec(rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), "all") +
                  ogl(conc_group) + disp("power"),
                data = one_plate, family = gfam,
                seed = 228, refresh = 0)
```

```{r candidates-conc-group, include = FALSE}
n_candidates <- length(fit_ogl$success_models)
```

`screen_models()` tests each equation of a set against R-hat, the effective
sample size and the number of divergent transitions, drops those that fail, and
recomputes the model weights over the rest (section 3 of [A complete analysis
workflow][e9] gives the cut-offs). We read all estimates from the screened sets.

```{r quiet-rstan, include = FALSE}
invisible(requireNamespace("rstan", quietly = TRUE))
```

```{r screen-conc-group, message = TRUE}
fit_plain <- screen_models(fit_plain)
fit_ogl <- screen_models(fit_ogl)
```

```{r weights-conc-group, include = FALSE}
w_plain <- set_weights(fit_plain)
w_ogl <- set_weights(fit_ogl)
top_ogl <- names(which.max(w_ogl))
k_top_ogl <- fit_ogl$mod_fits[[top_ogl]]$fit$criteria$logo$diagnostics$pareto_k
```

The screen dropped `r n_candidates - length(w_plain)` of the `r n_candidates`
equations from the ungrouped fit and `r n_candidates - length(w_ogl)` from the
grouped one. Because the four wells of a cell share one deviation, the weights
of `fit_ogl` are computed by leaving out each cell whole (its summary prints
"each level of conc_group left out"), whereas those of `fit_plain` leave out one
observation at a time, so weights are compared within a fit and not between
these fits. The last message of the screen notes that the left-out score is
approximate (Pareto *k* at or above 0.7) for `r sum(k_top_ogl >= 0.7)` of the 11 cells
under `r eq(top_ogl)`, and `loo_controls = list(refit = TRUE)` would replace
those scores by refitting the equation without each such cell.

`group_level_sd()` returns the SD of each group-level term for each equation,
beside that equation's weight. Each equation estimates its own SD, so an SD is
read against the weight of the equation that estimated it: an equation given
almost no weight describes its own curve and says little about the model
average.

```{r sd-conc-group}
sd_ogl <- group_level_sd(fit_ogl)
sd_ogl[sd_ogl$wi >= 0.01, ]
ec10_plain <- ecx(fit_plain, ecx_val = 10)
ec10_ogl <- ecx(fit_ogl, ecx_val = 10)
nsec_plain <- nsec(fit_plain)
nsec_ogl <- nsec(fit_ogl)
```

`lum31` comes from an acute assay, in which luminescence is read 15 and 30
minutes after exposure. Low-effect and no-effect estimates such as the EC10 and
NSEC are not usually appropriate endpoints for such an assay, and we report them
here only to illustrate the methods. The table below gives each fit's EC10 and
NSEC, and the weight, EC10, NSEC and cell SD of each equation given at least
0.01 of the weight.

```{r table-conc-group, echo = FALSE}
# The EC10 and NSEC of each equation given at least min_wi of the weight.
eq_estimates <- function(x, min_wi = 0.01) {
  w <- set_weights(x)
  keep <- names(sort(w[w >= min_wi], decreasing = TRUE))
  lapply(stats::setNames(keep, keep), function(m) {
    fit_m <- pull_out(x, model = m)
    list(ec10 = ecx(fit_m, ecx_val = 10), nsec = nsec(fit_m))
  })
}
eq_plain <- eq_estimates(fit_plain)
eq_ogl <- eq_estimates(fit_ogl)
ec10_eq_plain <- lapply(eq_plain, `[[`, "ec10")
ec10_eq_ogl <- lapply(eq_ogl, `[[`, "ec10")
weight_rows <- function(x, label, ec10, nsec, by_eq, sd = NULL) {
  keep <- names(by_eq)
  sd_cell <- if (is.null(sd)) {
    rep("", length(keep))
  } else {
    vapply(keep, function(m) ci(sd[sd$model == m, c("Estimate", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")]),
           character(1))
  }
  data.frame(
    fit = c(label, rep("", length(keep))),
    equation = c("model average", eq(keep)),
    weight = c("", sprintf("%.2f", set_weights(x)[keep])),
    ec10 = c(ci(ec10), vapply(by_eq, function(e) ci(e$ec10), character(1))),
    nsec = c(ci(nsec), vapply(by_eq, function(e) ci(e$nsec), character(1))),
    sd = c("", sd_cell)
  )
}
knitr::kable(
  rbind(weight_rows(fit_plain, "No term", ec10_plain, nsec_plain, eq_plain),
        weight_rows(fit_ogl, "`ogl(conc_group)`", ec10_ogl, nsec_ogl, eq_ogl,
                    sd = sd_ogl)),
  col.names = c("Fit", "Equation", "Weight", "EC10, mg/L", "NSEC, mg/L",
                "Cell SD"),
  row.names = FALSE
)
low_ogl <- setdiff(names(ec10_eq_ogl), top_ogl)
low_ec10 <- fmt(median(vapply(ec10_eq_ogl[low_ogl], function(v) v[[1]],
                              numeric(1))))
```

`autoplot()` with `group = "conc_group"` colours each well by its cell and, for
the fit with the term, marks the mean of each cell (diamonds), the quantity
`ogl(conc_group)` gives a deviation to. The legend is removed, because the
position of each cell on the axis already identifies it. `nec = TRUE`, the
default, would mark the model average of each equation's own no-effect
estimate, which is an NEC for an equation of the `nec` group, so we set it to
`FALSE`. We then add the EC10 and NSEC of the table above with
`mark_estimate()`, which draws an estimate as `autoplot()` draws an ECx under
`ecx = TRUE`, so that the figures show the estimates given in the tables.

```{r estimate-marks}
# autoplot() draws an ECx in this blue.
ec10_colour <- "dodgerblue4"
nsec_colour <- "darkorange3"
# `est` holds Q50, Q2.5 and Q97.5, and a column for each facet variable.
mark_estimate <- function(est, name, colour, vjust) {
  est$label <- paste0(name, ": ", fmt(est$Q50), " (", fmt(est$Q2.5), "-",
                      fmt(est$Q97.5), ")")
  list(
    geom_vline(data = est, aes(xintercept = Q50), colour = colour,
               linewidth = 0.5),
    geom_vline(data = est, aes(xintercept = Q2.5), colour = colour,
               linetype = 2, linewidth = 0.2),
    geom_vline(data = est, aes(xintercept = Q97.5), colour = colour,
               linetype = 2, linewidth = 0.2),
    geom_text(data = est, aes(label = label), x = Inf, y = Inf, hjust = 1.1,
              vjust = vjust, size = 3, colour = colour)
  )
}
# One row of `est` from the vector ecx() or nsec() returns for a model set.
est_row <- function(v, ...) {
  v <- unname(unlist(v))[1:3]
  data.frame(..., Q50 = v[1], Q2.5 = v[2], Q97.5 = v[3])
}
```

```{r fig-conc-group, fig.show = "hold", out.width = "48%", fig.asp = 0.9, fig.cap = "The zinc plate Zn 23Apr24 Rep6B at 15 minutes, fitted without a group-level term and with ogl(conc_group). Each point is one well, coloured by its cell, and in the second panel each diamond marks a cell mean. The dashed black line is the model-averaged curve and the grey band its 95% credible interval. The blue lines mark the EC10 and the orange lines the NSEC, solid at the median and dashed at the 95% bounds."}
autoplot(fit_plain, group = "conc_group", group_aes = "colour", nec = FALSE,
         point_alpha = 0.5) +
  mark_estimate(est_row(nsec_plain), "NSEC", nsec_colour, vjust = 1.5) +
  mark_estimate(est_row(ec10_plain), "EC10", ec10_colour, vjust = 3.2) +
  log_x + rlu_axes + theme(legend.position = "none") +
  ggtitle("No term")
autoplot(fit_ogl, group = "conc_group", group_aes = "colour", nec = FALSE,
         point_alpha = 0.5) +
  mark_estimate(est_row(nsec_ogl), "NSEC", nsec_colour, vjust = 1.5) +
  mark_estimate(est_row(ec10_ogl), "EC10", ec10_colour, vjust = 3.2) +
  log_x + rlu_axes + theme(legend.position = "none") +
  ggtitle("ogl(conc_group)")
```

The median EC10 is `r fmt(ec10_plain[[1]])` mg/L without the term and
`r fmt(ec10_ogl[[1]])` with it, whereas the upper bound of its interval is
`r round(ec10_ogl[[3]] / ec10_plain[[3]], 1)` times as large with the term. Two
changes contribute (table above). First, the interval of `r eq(top_ogl)`, given
most of the weight in both fits, widens from `r ci(ec10_eq_plain[[top_ogl]])` to
`r ci(ec10_eq_ogl[[top_ogl]])` mg/L. With the term, the four wells of a cell
share a deviation, so the curve is informed by eleven cell means, each with its
own departure, and not by 44 wells treated as independent. On one plate there is
one cell per concentration, so each cell's deviation is also its concentration's
departure from the curve. Second, `r eqs(low_ogl)`, given
`r sprintf("%.2f", sum(w_ogl[low_ogl]))` of the weight together, place the EC10
near `r low_ec10` mg/L and set the upper tail of the averaged interval. A
model-averaged interval is therefore read against the weights and the estimates
of the equations behind it.

The EC10 is read where the curve has fallen 10 per cent below the fitted
control, whereas the NSEC is read where the curve falls below the 0.01 quantile
of the posterior of the control (the fitted mean at the lowest concentration),
so the NSEC also depends on how precisely the control is estimated. The term
changes the NSEC more than the EC10. The median NSEC is
`r fmt(nsec_plain[[1]])` mg/L without the term, below the EC10, and
`r fmt(nsec_ogl[[1]])` mg/L with it, above the EC10 (orange and blue lines in
the figure). Under `r eq(top_ogl)`, given most of the weight in both fits, it
changes from `r ci(eq_plain[[top_ogl]]$nsec)` to
`r ci(eq_ogl[[top_ogl]]$nsec)` mg/L, so the change does not arise from the
model weights. With the term, the lowest concentration is
represented by one cell with its own departure from the curve, so the control is
less precisely estimated (the band at the lowest concentration is wider in the
second panel), and the curve has to fall further before it crosses that
quantile.

# Grouping across the concentration series

A plate spans the whole concentration series, so, unlike a cell, each plate can
have a curve of its own. The figure of plate-relative means suggests that the
zinc plates differ in two ways: the plate reader's gain scales each plate's
whole curve by one factor, and once that factor is divided out, the plates still
appear to differ in where and how steeply their curves fall. We fitted the zinc
readings at 15 minutes with four plate structures, labelled A to D in the table
and figure below:

- (A) no plate term;
- (B) `ogl(plate)`, which multiplies each plate's curve by one factor, the form
  the plate reader's gain takes;
- (C) `pgl(plate)`, which also lets each plate's curve differ in position and
  slope;
- (D) `pgl(plate) + ogl(conc_group)`, which adds the cell term of the
  single-plate fit, for the reason given there.

In D each cell deviates from its own plate's curve and not from the population
curve. Without a deviation on the position of each plate's curve (e.g. with
`ogl(plate) + ogl(conc_group)`), the cell deviations would also have to describe
each plate's shift along the concentration axis. We call D the arm structure,
because it is the structure fitted to each metal below.

A plate spans the series, so `ecx()` and `nsec()` read a fit with a plate term
from each observed plate's own curve, with all of that plate's deviations, and
measure each plate against its own control (its fitted mean at its lowest
concentration). The plates are then pooled by mixing their posterior draws in
proportion to each plate's number of observations, so the estimate describes
the `r n_distinct(zn15$plate)` plates that were tested and not a new plate. The
band that `autoplot()` and `ggbnec_data()` draw is a mixture of the plates'
curves in the same proportions. `pool` selects another combination (e.g. one estimate per plate
with `pool = "none"`), and `x_control` sets the concentration at which each
plate's control is read (see `?ecx`). The cell term in D is set to zero in
these predictions, as on the single plate, because each cell is observed at one
concentration, so its deviation remains part of the uncertainty in the control.

```{r fit-plate, results = "hide"}
fit_pooled <- bnec(rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), "all") +
                     disp("power"),
                   data = zn15, family = gfam,
                   seed = 228, refresh = 0)

fit_plate <- bnec(rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), "all") +
                    ogl(plate) + disp("power"),
                  data = zn15, family = gfam,
                  seed = 228, refresh = 0)
```

```{r fit-pgl, results = "hide"}
fit_pgl <- bnec(rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), "all") +
                  pgl(plate) + disp("power"),
                data = zn15, family = gfam,
                seed = 228, refresh = 0)
```

```{r fit-pgl-cell, results = "hide"}
fit_pgl_cell <- bnec(rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), "all") +
                       pgl(plate) + ogl(conc_group) + disp("power"),
                     data = zn15, family = gfam,
                     seed = 228, refresh = 0)
```

```{r sampling-plate, include = FALSE}
n_candidates_plate <- length(fit_pooled$success_models)
pgl_sampling <- check_sampling(fit_pgl)
pgl_stuck <- pgl_sampling[pgl_sampling$max_rhat > 1.1, ]
pgl_divergent <- pgl_sampling[pgl_sampling$max_rhat <= 1.1 & pgl_sampling$failed, ]
# The mean log density of each chain, read before the screen drops these fits.
chain_lp <- lapply(pgl_stuck$model, function(m) {
  d <- brms::as_draws_df(fit_pgl$mod_fits[[m]]$fit, variable = "lp__")
  tapply(d$lp__, d$.chain, mean)
})
n_apart <- vapply(chain_lp, function(lp) sum(abs(lp - median(lp)) > 10), integer(1))
apart_lower <- all(vapply(chain_lp, function(lp) {
  all(lp[abs(lp - median(lp)) > 10] < median(lp))
}, logical(1)))
```

```{r screen-plate}
fit_pooled <- screen_models(fit_pooled)
fit_plate <- screen_models(fit_plate)
fit_pgl <- screen_models(fit_pgl)
fit_pgl_cell <- screen_models(fit_pgl_cell)
```

The screen dropped `r nrow(pgl_stuck) + nrow(pgl_divergent)` equations from
`fit_pgl` (C). In each of `r eqs(pgl_stuck$model)`,
`r if (all(n_apart == 1)) "one" else paste(range(n_apart), collapse = " to ")`
of the four chains sampled a mode of its own, at a
`r if (apart_lower) "lower" else "different"` log density than the others, which
gives R-hat values of up to `r round(max(pgl_stuck$max_rhat), 2)` and effective sample
sizes as low as `r round(min(pgl_stuck$min_ess))`. The screen also dropped
`r eqs(pgl_divergent$model)` on
`r paste(pgl_divergent$n_divergent, collapse = ", ")` divergent transitions. The
table below gives the number of equations each structure retained, the weights,
and the EC10 and NSEC of each model average, and the figure that follows shows
each fit.

```{r table-plate, echo = FALSE}
plate_fits <- list(fit_pooled, fit_plate, fit_pgl, fit_pgl_cell)
plate_ec10 <- lapply(plate_fits, ecx, ecx_val = 10)
plate_nsec <- lapply(plate_fits, nsec)
knitr::kable(
  data.frame(
    panel = LETTERS[1:4],
    structure = c("None", "`ogl(plate)`", "`pgl(plate)`",
                  "`pgl(plate) + ogl(conc_group)`"),
    retained = paste(vapply(plate_fits, function(x) length(x$success_models),
                            integer(1)), "of", n_candidates_plate),
    weights = vapply(plate_fits, function(x) {
      w <- set_weights(x)
      w <- sort(w[w >= 0.05], decreasing = TRUE)
      paste0("`", names(w), "` ", sprintf("%.2f", w), collapse = ", ")
    }, character(1)),
    ec10 = vapply(plate_ec10, ci, character(1)),
    nsec = vapply(plate_nsec, ci, character(1))
  ),
  col.names = c("Panel", "Plate structure", "Equations retained",
                "Weight of 0.05 or more", "EC10, mg/L", "NSEC, mg/L")
)
ec10_range <- range(vapply(plate_ec10, function(v) v[[1]], numeric(1)))
nsec_range <- range(vapply(plate_nsec, function(v) v[[1]], numeric(1)))
# The ratio of the upper to the lower bound of each EC10 interval.
ec10_span <- vapply(plate_ec10, function(v) v[[3]] / v[[2]], numeric(1))
w_pgl <- set_weights(fit_pgl)
w_arm <- set_weights(fit_pgl_cell)
top_arm <- names(which.max(w_arm))
```

```{r fig-plate-terms, fig.width = 8, fig.asp = 0.85, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "The zinc readings at 15 minutes fitted with each plate structure (panels A to D). Each point is one well, and each thin line joins one plate's observed means, coloured by plate; these do not change between panels. The dashed black line is the median of the model-averaged curve and the grey band its 95% credible interval, taken in panels B to D over the mixture of the observed plates' curves. The blue lines mark the EC10 and the orange lines the NSEC, solid at the median and dashed at the 95% bounds."}
plate_panels <- c("(A) No plate term", "(B) ogl(plate)", "(C) pgl(plate)",
                  "(D) pgl(plate) + ogl(conc_group)")
plate_plot <- do.call(rbind, Map(function(x, panel) {
  d <- ggbnec_data(x, add_nec = FALSE, group = "plate")
  d$panel <- panel
  d
}, plate_fits, plate_panels))
plate_plot$panel <- factor(plate_plot$panel, levels = plate_panels)
plate_band <- filter(plate_plot, !is.na(y_ci))
plate_obs <- filter(plate_plot, !is.na(y_r))
by_panel <- function(est) {
  do.call(rbind, Map(est_row, est,
                     panel = factor(plate_panels, levels = plate_panels)))
}

ggplot() +
  geom_polygon(data = plate_band, aes(x = x_e, y = y_ci), fill = "grey75",
               alpha = 0.5) +
  geom_point(data = plate_obs, aes(x = x_r, y = y_r, colour = group),
             alpha = 0.3, size = 0.6) +
  stat_summary(data = plate_obs, aes(x = x_r, y = y_r, colour = group,
                                     group = group),
               fun = mean, geom = "line", linewidth = 0.3) +
  geom_line(data = filter(plate_band, !is.na(y_e)), aes(x = x_e, y = y_e),
            linetype = 2) +
  mark_estimate(by_panel(plate_nsec), "NSEC", nsec_colour, vjust = 1.5) +
  mark_estimate(by_panel(plate_ec10), "EC10", ec10_colour, vjust = 3.2) +
  facet_wrap(~panel, ncol = 2) +
  log_x + rlu_axes + theme_classic() +
  theme(legend.position = "none", strip.background = element_blank(),
        strip.text = element_text(hjust = 0))
```

```{r bands-plate, include = FALSE}
# The ratio of the upper to the lower bound of each band at the lowest
# concentration.
band_span <- vapply(plate_fits, function(x) {
  d <- x$w_pred_vals$data[which.min(x$w_pred_vals$data$x), ]
  d$Q97.5 / d$Q2.5
}, numeric(1))
widest_ec10 <- which.max(ec10_span)
ec10_factor <- ec10_range[2] / ec10_range[1]
nsec_factor <- nsec_range[2] / nsec_range[1]
change_text <- if (nsec_factor > 1.25 * ec10_factor) {
  "so the NSEC changed more with the structure than the EC10"
} else if (ec10_factor > 1.25 * nsec_factor) {
  "so the EC10 changed more with the structure than the NSEC"
} else {
  "so the EC10 and the NSEC changed by similar factors"
}
nsec_below <- mapply(function(n, e) n[[1]] < e[[1]], plate_nsec, plate_ec10)
nsec_text <- if (all(nsec_below)) {
  "In each panel the median NSEC is below the median EC10"
} else {
  paste0("The median NSEC is below the median EC10 in ",
         if (any(nsec_below)) paste("panels", paste(LETTERS[1:4][nsec_below],
                                                    collapse = ", "))
         else "no panel",
         " and above it in ",
         paste("panels", paste(LETTERS[1:4][!nsec_below], collapse = ", ")))
}
plate_nsec_text <- paste(
  "Under a plate term, each plate's control is read from that plate's own",
  "curve, which the plate's wells determine closely, so the 0.01 quantile of",
  "the posterior of its control is close to the fitted control, and the curve",
  "crosses it before it has fallen by 10 per cent. A plate term therefore does",
  "not place the NSEC above the EC10, as the cell term did on the single plate,",
  "because a plate's deviations apply to its control and to each of its",
  "treatments, whereas each cell has a deviation of its own."
)
# The ratio of the median NSEC to the median EC10 in each panel.
nsec_ec10 <- mapply(function(n, e) n[[1]] / e[[1]], plate_nsec, plate_ec10)
if (which.max(nsec_ec10) == 4) {
  plate_nsec_text <- paste0(
    plate_nsec_text, " The median NSEC is closest to the median EC10 in ",
    "panel D (a ratio of ", fmt(nsec_ec10[4]), ", against ",
    fmt(min(nsec_ec10)), " to ", fmt(max(nsec_ec10[-4])), " in the other ",
    "panels), probably because its cell term adds the cells' deviations to ",
    "the uncertainty in the control."
  )
}
```

The observations, and the thin lines that join each plate's observed means, do
not change between panels. The panels differ only in the fitted curve, its band,
and the EC10 and NSEC.

Without a plate term (panel A), the differences in gain between plates are
counted as residual variation, and one curve is fitted to the wells of all
`r n_distinct(zn15$plate)` plates as though they came from one plate. Its band
is therefore narrow, with an upper bound `r round(band_span[1], 2)` times its
lower bound at the lowest concentration. With a plate term (panels B to D), the
band is that of the mixture of the plates' curves, so at the lowest
concentration it includes the differences in gain between plates as well as the
uncertainty in each plate's curve, and its upper bound is
`r fmt(min(band_span[-1]))` to `r fmt(max(band_span[-1]))` times its lower
bound.

The EC10 interval depends on the kind of plate term. `ogl(plate)` (B) scales
each plate's curve by one factor, which cancels in an effect measured from that
plate's own control, so in each draw the plates' EC10s differ little, and the
upper bound of the interval is `r round(ec10_span[2], 1)` times its lower
bound. Under `pgl(plate)` (C and D), the plates' curves also differ in position
and slope, so the interval includes the differences between the plates' EC10s,
and the ratio is `r round(ec10_span[3], 1)` and `r round(ec10_span[4], 1)`,
respectively. Without a plate term (A), the ratio is
`r round(ec10_span[1], 1)``r if (widest_ec10 == 1) ", the widest of the four, probably because the differences between plates are then counted as residual variation" else ""`.

The four structures also give the curve different positions. Across the panels
the median EC10 ranges from `r fmt(ec10_range[1])` to `r fmt(ec10_range[2])`
mg/L (a factor of `r fmt(ec10_factor)`) and the median NSEC from
`r fmt(nsec_range[1])` to `r fmt(nsec_range[2])` (a factor of
`r fmt(nsec_factor)`), `r change_text`. `r nsec_text` (orange and blue lines in
the figure). `r if (all(nsec_below[-1])) plate_nsec_text else ""` Because the estimates
differ between structures, a report should state the structure as well as the
estimate. The weights changed as well, and no
equation was given most of the weight under every structure. Under
`pgl(plate)` (panel C) the weight was spread over `r sum(w_pgl >= 0.05)` equations and the EC10 was
`r ci(plate_ec10[[3]])` mg/L, whereas adding the cell term (panel D) gave
`r eq(top_arm)` `r sprintf("%.2f", w_arm[[top_arm]])` of the weight and an EC10
of `r ci(plate_ec10[[4]])` mg/L.

The table below gives the group-level SDs of `fit_pgl_cell` (structure D) from
`group_level_sd()`, for each equation given at least 0.01 of the weight. Each SD
measures how much the plates, or the cells within a plate, differ in one
parameter.

```{r sd-plate, echo = FALSE}
sd_arm <- group_level_sd(fit_pgl_cell)
sd_arm <- sd_arm[sd_arm$wi >= 0.01, ]
sd_pgl <- group_level_sd(fit_pgl)
sd_pgl_ec50 <- sd_pgl[sd_pgl$wi >= 0.05 & sd_pgl$parameter == "ec50", ]
sd_top <- function(parameter) {
  sd_arm$Estimate[sd_arm$model == top_arm & sd_arm$parameter == parameter]
}
cell_pct <- round(100 * sd_top("ogl"))
gain_factor <- exp(sd_top("topgl"))
sd_of <- function(tab, model, parameter) {
  ci(tab[tab$model == model & tab$parameter == parameter,
         c("Estimate", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
}
knitr::kable(
  data.frame(equation = eq(sd_arm$model), weight = sprintf("%.2f", sd_arm$wi),
             group = eq(sd_arm$group), parameter = eq(sd_arm$parameter),
             sd = vapply(seq_len(nrow(sd_arm)), function(i) {
               ci(sd_arm[i, c("Estimate", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
             }, character(1))),
  col.names = c("Equation", "Weight", "Grouping", "Parameter", "SD")
)
```

`r eq(top_arm)` is given `r sprintf("%.2f", w_arm[[top_arm]])` of the weight
under structure D, so its SDs describe the model average. Its SD on `topgl`,
`r sd_of(sd_arm, top_arm, "topgl")`, measures the differences in gain between
plates on the log scale of a multiplicative factor, so the control of a plate
one SD above the population is about `r fmt(gain_factor)` times as high. The
plates also appear to differ in position along the concentration axis, which
`ogl(plate)` (B) cannot describe, because it only scales each plate's curve.
Under `pgl(plate)` without the cell term (C), each equation given at least 0.05
of the weight estimates an SD on `ec50` of `r range_text(sd_pgl_ec50$Estimate)`
on the scale of log concentration. Under D, however, `ecxll5` appears to express
that difference through its exponent `f`, which also changes the position of
the curve, with an SD of `r sd_of(sd_arm, "ecxll5", "f")`, whereas its SD on
`ec50` is poorly determined (`r sd_of(sd_arm, "ecxll5", "ec50")`). The cell SD,
`r sd_of(sd_arm, top_arm, "ogl")`, corresponds to departures of about
`r cell_pct` per cent between the cells of a plate beyond what the plate's curve
accounts for, which is small beside the SD on `topgl`.

Two cautions apply to plate terms. First, the SD of a plate term combines the
plate reader's gain with any biological difference between plates, and nothing
in these data separates gain from biology, so it is not a biological quantity
and should not be reported as one. Second, a plate term belongs within one arm:
copper against zinc, or 15 against 30 minutes, are fixed levels, for
`bnec_group()` or `bnec_joint()` (see below).

# Copper against zinc

Copper and zinc are fixed levels (see *Types of grouping*), so we fit them with
`bnec_group()`. Although both metals are passed to one call, `bnec_group()`
fits them independently: it calls `bnec()` once for each level, on that level's
data alone, and returns both model sets as one object. Each metal therefore
has its own curve parameters, group-level SDs, dispersion and model weights, and
no parameter is shared between them. Each level is fitted with the arm
structure, `pgl(plate) + ogl(conc_group)`, and with `model = "all"`, because
zinc rises above its control at low concentrations.

```{r fit-metals, results = "hide"}
fits_tox <- bnec_group(
  rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), "all") +
    pgl(plate) + ogl(conc_group) + disp("power"),
  data = zn_cu_15, group_var = "toxicant", family = gfam,
  seed = 228, refresh = 0)
```

Because each metal's model set was fitted independently, its sampling is also
screened independently. With `group_action = "per_level"`, an equation that
fails the screen for one metal is dropped for that metal only, so copper and
zinc may retain different equations.

```{r screen-metals}
fits_tox <- screen_models(fits_tox, group_action = "per_level")
```

```{r table-metals, echo = FALSE}
level_fits <- stats::setNames(fits_tox$fits, fits_tox$levels)
level_weights <- lapply(level_fits, set_weights)
level_ec10 <- ecx(fits_tox, ecx_val = 10)
level_nsec <- nsec(fits_tox)
level_top <- vapply(level_weights, function(w) names(which.max(w)), character(1))
k_top <- mapply(function(f, m) {
  sum(f$mod_fits[[m]]$fit$criteria$logo$diagnostics$pareto_k >= 0.7)
}, level_fits, level_top)
level_text <- function(tab, l) {
  ci(tab[tab$level == l, c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
}
# Places each metal's median NSEC against its median EC10, reading a
# difference of under 5 per cent as none.
nsec_place <- vapply(c(Cu = "Cu", Zn = "Zn"), function(l) {
  r <- level_nsec$Q50[level_nsec$level == l] /
    level_ec10$Q50[level_ec10$level == l]
  if (abs(log(r)) < log(1.05)) "within 5 per cent of" else
    if (r < 1) "below" else "above"
}, character(1))
nsec_place_text <- if (identical(nsec_place[["Cu"]], nsec_place[["Zn"]])) {
  paste(nsec_place[["Cu"]], "the median EC10 for both metals")
} else {
  paste(nsec_place[["Cu"]], "the median EC10 for copper and",
        nsec_place[["Zn"]], "it for zinc")
}
metal_span <- vapply(c(Cu = "Cu", Zn = "Zn"), function(l) {
  r <- level_ec10[level_ec10$level == l, ]
  r$Q97.5 / r$Q2.5
}, numeric(1))
knitr::kable(
  data.frame(
    level = fits_tox$levels,
    retained = paste(vapply(level_weights, length, integer(1)), "of",
                     n_candidates_plate),
    weights = vapply(level_weights, function(w) {
      w <- sort(w[w >= 0.01], decreasing = TRUE)
      paste0("`", names(w), "` ", sprintf("%.2f", w), collapse = ", ")
    }, character(1)),
    ec10 = vapply(fits_tox$levels, level_text, character(1), tab = level_ec10),
    nsec = vapply(fits_tox$levels, level_text, character(1), tab = level_nsec)
  ),
  col.names = c("Toxicant", "Equations retained", "Weight of 0.01 or more",
                "EC10, mg/L", "NSEC, mg/L"),
  row.names = FALSE
)
```

`autoplot()` draws one panel for each level of a `bnec_group()` fit and
identifies the panels by a column named `model`, so a layer added to the plot
needs that column to be drawn in the right panel. We add the EC10 and NSEC of
each metal from the table above in this way.

```{r fig-metals, fig.asp = 0.5, out.width = "95%", fig.cap = "The copper and zinc readings at 15 minutes, with the model-averaged curve of each metal's separate fit (dashed line, the median over the mixture of that metal's plates) and its 95% credible interval (grey band). The blue lines mark the EC10 and the orange lines the NSEC, solid at the median and dashed at the 95% bounds."}
by_level <- function(tab) {
  data.frame(model = paste0(fits_tox$group_var, " = ", tab$level),
             tab[, c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
}
autoplot(fits_tox, nec = FALSE, point_alpha = 0.5) +
  mark_estimate(by_level(level_nsec), "NSEC", nsec_colour, vjust = 1.5) +
  mark_estimate(by_level(level_ec10), "EC10", ec10_colour, vjust = 3.2) +
  log_x + rlu_axes
```

The screen retained `r length(level_weights[["Cu"]])` equations for copper and
`r length(level_weights[["Zn"]])` for zinc, and the equation given the most
weight was `r if (identical(level_top[["Cu"]], level_top[["Zn"]])) paste(eq(level_top[["Cu"]]), "at both levels") else paste(eq(level_top[["Cu"]]), "for copper and", eq(level_top[["Zn"]]), "for zinc")`
(table above). The EC10 is `r level_text(level_ec10, "Cu")` mg/L for copper
and `r level_text(level_ec10, "Zn")` mg/L for zinc, so copper is the more potent
metal, as @luter2025 report. The NSEC is `r level_text(level_nsec, "Cu")` mg/L
for copper and `r level_text(level_nsec, "Zn")` mg/L for zinc, so its median is
`r nsec_place_text` (orange and blue lines in the figure). The upper bound of
copper's EC10 interval is `r fmt(metal_span[["Cu"]])` times its lower bound,
against `r fmt(metal_span[["Zn"]])` for zinc`r if (metal_span[["Cu"]] > 1.3 * metal_span[["Zn"]]) ", most likely because the mixture over copper's plates includes the two 1Oct24 plates, whose curves fall at higher concentrations than those of the other copper plates (figure of plate-relative means)" else ""`.
Each panel has its own response axis, because the plates of each metal were
read at different gains, and their relative light units are not comparable
between metals. Under the equation given the most weight, the
left-out score is approximate (Pareto *k* at or above 0.7) for `r k_top[["Cu"]]` of
the 176 copper cells and `r k_top[["Zn"]]` of the 187 zinc cells, so these
weights are approximate as well, and `loo_controls = list(refit = TRUE)` would
replace the flagged scores by refitting the equation once for each flagged cell.

`check_fit()` compares the observed mean and residual SD of each group of
readings with those of responses simulated from each equation of a set, and on
a `bnec_group()` fit it computes the check within each level. Each batch was
dosed separately, so we group the readings by dilution step (`conc_rank`) and
not by concentration, and a censored reading is held at its bound in both the
observed and the simulated responses (see `?check_fit`). The rows below are
those of the equation given the most weight for copper: the number of censored
readings at each step, and the ratio of the observed to the simulated mean and
residual SD, each with its posterior predictive p-value.

```{r check-metals}
checks_tox <- check_fit(fits_tox, group = zn_cu_15$conc_rank, ndraws = 1000,
                        seed = 228)
cu_check <- as.data.frame(checks_tox$fits[["Cu"]])
print(cu_check[cu_check$model == level_top[["Cu"]],
               c("group", "n_cens", "mean_ratio", "ppp_mean", "sd_ratio",
                 "ppp_sd")], digits = 2, row.names = FALSE)
```

```{r check-metals-summary, include = FALSE}
# The check rows of the equation given the most weight at each level.
check_top <- lapply(stats::setNames(fits_tox$levels, fits_tox$levels),
                    function(l) {
  d <- checks_tox$fits[[l]]
  d[d$model == level_top[[l]], ]
})
# The dilution steps at which an observed statistic is outside the 95 per
# cent span of its simulated values, below or above it.
steps_out <- function(d, stat, side = c("any", "below", "above")) {
  side <- match.arg(side)
  obs <- d[[paste0("obs_", stat)]]
  lo <- obs < d[[paste0("sim_", stat, "_lo")]]
  hi <- obs > d[[paste0("sim_", stat, "_hi")]]
  out <- switch(side, any = which(lo | hi), below = which(lo),
                above = which(hi))
  as.integer(as.character(d$group[out]))
}
step_text <- function(s) {
  if (!length(s)) return("no step")
  s <- as.character(s)
  if (length(s) == 1) return(paste("step", s))
  paste("steps", paste(s[-length(s)], collapse = ", "), "and", s[length(s)])
}
mean_out <- lapply(check_top, steps_out, "mean")
sd_text <- vapply(check_top, function(d) {
  below <- steps_out(d, "sd", "below")
  above <- steps_out(d, "sd", "above")
  if (!length(below) && !length(above)) return("at no step")
  paste0(if (length(below)) paste("below the simulated span at",
                                  step_text(below)),
         if (length(below) && length(above)) " and ",
         if (length(above)) paste("above it at", step_text(above)))
}, character(1))
cu_top <- check_top[["Cu"]]
cu_mean_steps <- mean_out[["Cu"]]
cu_censored <- cu_top$n_cens[cu_top$group %in% cu_mean_steps]
cu_step_conc <- with(subset(zn_cu_15, toxicant == "Cu"),
                     tapply(conc, conc_rank, median))
cu_control_sd_high <- 1 %in% steps_out(cu_top, "sd", "below")
and_text <- function(x) {
  if (length(x) < 2) return(as.character(x))
  paste(paste(x[-length(x)], collapse = ", "), "and", x[length(x)])
}
cu_control_text <- if (cu_control_sd_high) {
  paste("At copper's control the simulated SD is larger than the observed one,",
        "and *Why the control row matters* in `?check_fit` explains how an",
        "overstated spread at the control can place the NSEC at a higher",
        "concentration.")
} else {
  ""
}
cu_floor_text <- if (length(cu_mean_steps)) {
  paste0(" (from ", fmt(min(cu_step_conc[as.character(cu_mean_steps)])),
         " mg/L, against an EC10 of ",
         fmt(level_ec10$Q50[level_ec10$level == "Cu"]), " mg/L), where ",
         and_text(cu_censored), " of the ", cu_top$n[1], " readings",
         if (length(cu_censored) > 1) ", respectively," else "",
         " are censored at the instrument's floor")
} else {
  ""
}
```

For zinc, under `r eq(level_top[["Zn"]])`, the observed mean is within the
simulated span at `r if (!length(mean_out[["Zn"]])) "every dilution step" else paste("every step except", step_text(mean_out[["Zn"]]))`,
and the residual SD is `r sd_text[["Zn"]]`. For copper, the observed mean is
outside the simulated span at `r step_text(cu_mean_steps)``r cu_floor_text`,
and the residual SD is `r sd_text[["Cu"]]`. `r if (length(cu_mean_steps) && all(cu_mean_steps >= 7)) paste0(eq(level_top[["Cu"]]), " therefore does not follow copper's readings as they approach the floor, well above the concentrations at which the EC10 and NSEC are read.") else ""`
`r cu_control_text`

Each metal's model average is formed over its own equations, so a different
equation may describe each metal. Because the metals share no parameter, the
model weight of a combination of one copper equation and one zinc equation is
the product of their weights at their own levels, under the pseudo-BMA weights
`bnec()` uses by default. `crossed_group_weights()` returns the weights of each
level (`per_level`), the combination given the most weight
(`best_combination`), and the weight of each equation retained at both levels
when that one equation is fitted to both metals (`diagonal`, the combinations
that pair an equation with itself, rescaled to sum to one).

```{r crossed-metals}
crossed <- crossed_group_weights(fits_tox)
crossed$best_combination
round(crossed$diagonal[crossed$diagonal >= 0.001], 3)
```

The table below gives the crossed weight of each combination of the equations
given at least 0.01 of the weight at each level, with each equation's own weight
in parentheses.

```{r table-crossed, echo = FALSE}
w_cross <- lapply(stats::setNames(crossed$per_level, fits_tox$levels),
                  function(w) sort(w[w >= 0.01], decreasing = TRUE))
cross_tab <- outer(w_cross[["Cu"]], w_cross[["Zn"]])
eq_weight <- function(w) paste0(eq(names(w)), " (", sprintf("%.2f", w), ")")
knitr::kable(
  data.frame(copper = eq_weight(w_cross[["Cu"]]),
             matrix(sprintf("%.3f", cross_tab), nrow = nrow(cross_tab))),
  col.names = c("Copper equation (weight)",
                paste("Zinc", eq_weight(w_cross[["Zn"]]))),
  row.names = FALSE
)
best_text <- with(as.list(crossed$best_combination), {
  if (identical(Cu, Zn)) paste(eq(Cu), "to both metals") else
    paste(eq(Cu), "to copper and", eq(Zn), "to zinc")
})
```

The combination given the most crossed weight,
`r sprintf("%.2f", crossed$best_weight)`, fits `r best_text`. Most of the
remaining weight is given to combinations of zinc's `r eq(level_top[["Zn"]])`
with another copper equation, because copper's weight is divided among more
equations than zinc's. On the diagonal, `ecxll5` is given
`r sprintf("%.3f", crossed$diagonal[["ecxll5"]])` of the weight over the
`r length(crossed$common_models)` equations retained for both metals. The crossed
weights do not change either metal's model average, which is formed over that
metal's own equations. They show instead which single equation best describes
both metals, the choice that a joint refit of copper and zinc requires (see
below).

# Joint refit of copper and zinc

Each of the five batches includes plates of both metals (two to five of each),
which were dosed and read on one date, and the figure of plate-relative means
shows the 1Oct24 plates of each metal displaced to higher concentrations, the
copper plates clearly and the zinc plates less so. A
deviation on `ec50` for each batch, shared by the metals, is the term for such a
displacement. The separate fits of `fits_tox` cannot include it, because each
level sees only its own metal's plates, so a batch deviation would be estimated
twice and independently. `bnec_joint()` refits the levels of a `bnec_group()`
fit as one model, in which each level keeps its own curve parameters and a
group-level term can span the levels (see `?bnec_joint`).

Without `model`, `bnec_joint()` fits each level the equation its own weights
favour, which may differ between levels. A group-level term that spans the
levels, however, can be written only on a parameter that each level's equation
includes, so `model` names one equation to be fitted to both metals. We use
`ecxll5`, the equation given the most weight at both levels
(`r sprintf("%.2f", level_weights[["Cu"]][["ecxll5"]])` for copper and
`r sprintf("%.2f", level_weights[["Zn"]][["ecxll5"]])` for zinc) and on the
diagonal of the crossed weights
(`r sprintf("%.3f", crossed$diagonal[["ecxll5"]])`). The formula adds
`(ec50 | batch)` to the structure of `fits_tox` and writes its plate and cell
terms as `pgl(gr(plate, by = toxicant))` and `ogl(gr(conc_group, by = toxicant))`.
`gr()` is the `brms` function that declares a grouping, and with
`by = toxicant` a separate SD is fitted for each metal (see `?brms::gr`).
`disp_by_level = FALSE` fits one dispersion sub-model for both metals, where the
default would fit one for each level:

```{r fit-metals-joint, results = "hide"}
fits_tox_joint <- bnec_joint(
  fits_tox, model = "ecxll5",
  formula = rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), "ecxll5") +
    pgl(gr(plate, by = toxicant)) + ogl(gr(conc_group, by = toxicant)) +
    (ec50 | batch) + disp("power"),
  disp_by_level = FALSE, seed = 228, refresh = 0)
```

```{r print-metals-joint}
fits_tox_joint
```

```{r weight-note-joint, include = FALSE}
recorded_weight <- stats::setNames(unname(fits_tox_joint$level_weights),
                                   fits_tox_joint$levels)
shown_weight <- vapply(level_weights[names(recorded_weight)],
                       function(w) w[["ecxll5"]], numeric(1))
weight_note <- if (any(sprintf("%.2f", recorded_weight) !=
                       sprintf("%.2f", shown_weight))) {
  paste("Those weights were computed when the refit was made, and differ",
        "slightly from the ones given above for the same fits, because",
        "pseudo-BMA weights are computed by a Bayesian bootstrap over the",
        "leave-out scores.")
} else {
  ""
}
```

The print gives the equation fitted at each level and the weight it was given
there. `r weight_note`
The `brms` formula of the refit shows which terms the metals share:

```{r formula-metals-joint}
fits_tox_joint$fit$formula
```

Each curve parameter of `ecxll5` takes a separate value for each metal
(`0 + toxicant`). The metals share the five batch deviations of `(1 | batch)` on
`ec50` and the coefficients `c0` and `c1` of `disp("power")`. Each plate and
cell belongs to one metal, and its deviation is drawn from an SD estimated from
that metal's plates or cells alone, as in the separate fits: one SD per
parameter for `plate` and one for `conc_group`. The table below gives the SDs
of the joint refit beside those of `ecxll5` in each level of `fits_tox`.

```{r sd-joint, echo = FALSE}
vc_joint <- brms::VarCorr(fits_tox_joint$fit, summary = FALSE)
# brms names the SD of a by term <parameter>_Intercept:<by variable><level>.
by_tag <- paste0(":", fits_tox_joint$group_var)
sd_joint <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(vc_joint), function(g) {
  d <- vc_joint[[g]]$sd
  per_level <- grepl(by_tag, colnames(d), fixed = TRUE)
  data.frame(group = g, parameter = sub("_Intercept.*$", "", colnames(d)),
             level = ifelse(per_level, sub(paste0("^.*", by_tag), "",
                                           colnames(d)), NA),
             Estimate = apply(d, 2, median),
             Q2.5 = apply(d, 2, quantile, probs = 0.025),
             Q97.5 = apply(d, 2, quantile, probs = 0.975), row.names = NULL)
}))
sd_joint <- sd_joint[order(match(sd_joint$group, unique(sd_joint$group)),
                           match(sd_joint$parameter, unique(sd_joint$parameter)),
                           sd_joint$level), ]
sd_tox <- group_level_sd(fits_tox)
sd_tox <- sd_tox[sd_tox$model == "ecxll5", ]
level_sd <- function(level, g, p) {
  r <- sd_tox[sd_tox$level == level & sd_tox$group == g & sd_tox$parameter == p, ]
  if (nrow(r) == 0) "not fitted" else ci(r[1, c("Estimate", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
}
metal_name <- c(Cu = "Copper", Zn = "Zinc")
knitr::kable(
  data.frame(
    group = eq(sd_joint$group), parameter = eq(sd_joint$parameter),
    metal = ifelse(is.na(sd_joint$level), "Both",
                   metal_name[sd_joint$level]),
    separate = vapply(seq_len(nrow(sd_joint)), function(i) {
      if (is.na(sd_joint$level[i])) return("not fitted")
      level_sd(sd_joint$level[i], sd_joint$group[i], sd_joint$parameter[i])
    }, character(1)),
    joint = vapply(seq_len(nrow(sd_joint)), function(i) {
      ci(sd_joint[i, c("Estimate", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
    }, character(1))
  ),
  col.names = c("Grouping", "Parameter", "Toxicant", "Separate fit",
                "Joint refit"),
  row.names = FALSE
)
batch_sd <- unlist(sd_joint[sd_joint$group == "batch", c("Estimate", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
# A level of NA selects a term the metals share; %in% matches NA to NA.
joint_sd_text <- function(g, p, level = NA) {
  ci(sd_joint[sd_joint$group == g & sd_joint$parameter == p &
                sd_joint$level %in% level, c("Estimate", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
}
cu_ec50_sep <- sd_tox[sd_tox$level == "Cu" & sd_tox$group == "plate" &
                        sd_tox$parameter == "ec50", ]
cu_ec50_joint <- sd_joint[sd_joint$group == "plate" &
                            sd_joint$parameter == "ec50" &
                            sd_joint$level %in% "Cu", ]
cu_ec50_text <- if (cu_ec50_joint$Estimate[1] < cu_ec50_sep$Q2.5[1]) {
  paste("Copper's plate SD on `ec50` in the joint refit is below the interval",
        "of its separate fit, so part of the difference in position that the",
        "copper fit attributed to plates appears to be attributed to batches",
        "here.")
} else {
  paste("Copper's plate SD on `ec50` in the joint refit is within or above the",
        "interval of its separate fit, so the difference in position that the",
        "copper fit attributed to plates does not appear to be attributed to",
        "batches here.")
}
```

The separate fits estimate different plate SDs for the two metals on some
parameters. For example, on `ec50` the SD is `r level_sd("Cu", "plate", "ec50")`
for copper and `r level_sd("Zn", "plate", "ec50")` for zinc, and on `f` it is
`r level_sd("Cu", "plate", "f")` and `r level_sd("Zn", "plate", "f")`. One SD
per parameter for both metals would assume that the plates of one metal vary as
much as those of the other, which these pairs of estimates suggest may not hold
for these data. In the joint refit the plate SD on `ec50` is
`r joint_sd_text("plate", "ec50", "Cu")` for copper and
`r joint_sd_text("plate", "ec50", "Zn")` for zinc, and on `f` it is
`r joint_sd_text("plate", "f", "Cu")` and `r joint_sd_text("plate", "f", "Zn")`.

A batch term shared by the metals is consistent with the caution above that a
plate term belongs within one arm. The metals keep separate curve parameters,
and the batch deviation describes a condition of the date on which both were
tested: the plates of both metals in a batch were dosed and read on one date,
with cells reactivated that day from the single batch of lyophilised cells used
for every test [@luter2025]. A
condition of that date (e.g. the state of the reactivated cells, or that day's
dilution series) would be expected to displace both metals' curves together. The
batch SD on `ec50` is `r ci(batch_sd)`, and five batches leave it poorly
determined (its interval spans a factor of
`r round(batch_sd[[3]] / batch_sd[[2]])` from end to end). `r cu_ec50_text`

The table below sets the EC50 of each metal from the joint refit beside the
model-averaged EC50 of the separate fits and the per-plate EC50s that @luter2025
report for 15 minutes.

```{r ec50-joint, echo = FALSE}
ec50_joint <- ecx(fits_tox_joint, ecx_val = 50)
ec50_sep <- ecx(fits_tox, ecx_val = 50)
ec50_text <- function(tab, l) ci(tab[tab$level == l, c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")])
knitr::kable(
  data.frame(
    toxicant = c("Copper", "Zinc"),
    joint = c(ec50_text(ec50_joint, "Cu"), ec50_text(ec50_joint, "Zn")),
    separate = c(ec50_text(ec50_sep, "Cu"), ec50_text(ec50_sep, "Zn")),
    published = c("0.109 (0.069--0.201; 16 plates)",
                  "1.546 (1.207--2.076; 17 plates)")
  ),
  col.names = c("Toxicant", "Joint refit", "Separate fits, model average",
                "Luter et al. (2025), mean of per-plate EC50s (range)"),
  row.names = FALSE
)
pub_range <- list(Cu = c(0.069, 0.201), Zn = c(1.207, 2.076))
within_pub <- all(mapply(function(m, l) {
  m >= pub_range[[l]][1] && m <= pub_range[[l]][2]
}, c(ec50_joint$Q50, ec50_sep$Q50),
   as.character(c(ec50_joint$level, ec50_sep$level))))
# The width of each joint EC50 interval relative to the separate fit's, as a
# ratio of the upper to the lower bound of each.
ec50_bounds <- function(tab, l) {
  r <- tab[tab$level == l, ]
  r$Q97.5 / r$Q2.5
}
span_ratio <- vapply(c("Cu", "Zn"), function(l) {
  ec50_bounds(ec50_joint, l) / ec50_bounds(ec50_sep, l)
}, numeric(1))
width_text <- if (all(abs(log(span_ratio)) < log(1.2))) {
  paste("similar in width to those of the separate fits, although the joint",
        "refit conditions on one equation")
} else if (all(span_ratio > 1)) {
  paste("wider than those of the separate fits, although the joint refit",
        "conditions on one equation")
} else if (all(span_ratio < 1)) {
  "narrower than those of the separate fits, which average several equations"
} else {
  "wider than those of the separate fits for one metal and narrower for the other"
}
```

The EC50 is `r ec50_text(ec50_joint, "Cu")` mg/L for copper and
`r ec50_text(ec50_joint, "Zn")` mg/L for zinc in the joint refit, against
`r ec50_text(ec50_sep, "Cu")` and `r ec50_text(ec50_sep, "Zn")` from the
separate fits, and
`r if (within_pub) "all four medians lie" else "not all four medians lie"`
within the range of the per-plate EC50s that @luter2025 report. The intervals of
the joint refit are `r width_text`. Both estimates are mixtures over each
metal's observed plates, so their intervals include the differences between
plates as well as the uncertainty in each plate's curve. The published values
are also read from each plate's own curve. However, they average a separate fit
to each plate of the normalised response, whereas `ecx()` mixes the plates'
curves from one model, in which the plates of a metal share information through
the plate SDs.

Because the levels are estimated in one posterior, `compare_posterior()`
subtracts their EC10s at matching draws and retains any dependence between the
metals' estimates that the shared terms introduce. We use the aligned draws it
returns to take the ratio of zinc's EC10 to copper's:

```{r ratio-joint}
cmp_joint <- compare_posterior(fits_tox_joint, comparison = "ecx", ecx_val = 10)
ec10_joint <- cmp_joint$posterior_list
zn_cu_ratio <- ec10_joint[["Zn"]] / ec10_joint[["Cu"]]
quantile(zn_cu_ratio, c(0.5, 0.025, 0.975), na.rm = TRUE)
```

```{r ratio-summary, include = FALSE}
# ecx() gives no value to a draw whose EC10 lies beyond the tested range.
ratio_censored <- if (any(is.na(zn_cu_ratio))) {
  paste0(" (leaving out ", sum(is.na(zn_cu_ratio)), " draws whose EC10 lies",
         " beyond the tested range)")
} else {
  ""
}
ratio_q <- quantile(zn_cu_ratio, c(0.5, 0.025, 0.975), na.rm = TRUE)
# The ratio of the upper to the lower bound of each 95 per cent interval.
level_span <- vapply(ec10_joint, function(d) {
  q <- quantile(d, c(0.025, 0.975), na.rm = TRUE)
  q[[2]] / q[[1]]
}, numeric(1))
log_cor <- cor(log(ec10_joint[["Zn"]]), log(ec10_joint[["Cu"]]),
               use = "complete.obs")
```

```{r fig-ratio-joint, fig.asp = 0.5, out.width = "70%"}
ggplot(data.frame(ratio = zn_cu_ratio), aes(x = ratio)) +
  geom_density(fill = "grey85", colour = "grey30") +
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log10") +
  theme_classic() +
  labs(x = "Ratio of zinc's EC10 to copper's (joint refit)", y = "Density")
```

Zinc's EC10 is `r ci(ratio_q)` times copper's`r ratio_censored`, and the ratio
exceeds 1 in `r ratio_above <- round(100 * mean(zn_cu_ratio > 1, na.rm = TRUE), 1); if (ratio_above == 100) "more than 99.9" else ratio_above` per cent of
the draws. The interval of the ratio spans a factor of
`r fmt(ratio_q[[3]] / ratio_q[[2]])` between its bounds, whereas the EC10
interval of each metal spans a factor of `r if (identical(fmt(level_span[["Cu"]]), fmt(level_span[["Zn"]]))) fmt(level_span[["Cu"]]) else paste(fmt(level_span[["Cu"]]), "for copper and", fmt(level_span[["Zn"]]), "for zinc")`. The metals' EC10s are correlated
across draws (`r fmt(log_cor)` on the log scale), most likely because the shared
batch deviations shift both curves together, so much of their uncertainty
cancels in the ratio. Each draw of the ratio is taken from one draw of the joint
posterior, which keeps that correlation, whereas the separate fits of
`fits_tox` share no parameter, so a ratio formed from their draws could not
include it.

```{r diagnostics-joint, include = FALSE}
np_joint <- brms::nuts_params(fits_tox_joint$fit)
s_joint <- summary(fits_tox_joint$fit)
pars_joint <- rbind(s_joint$fixed, s_joint$spec_pars,
                    do.call(rbind, s_joint$random))
joint_divergent <- sum(np_joint$Value[np_joint$Parameter == "divergent__"])
joint_draws <- sum(np_joint$Parameter == "divergent__")
joint_max_depth <- fits_tox_joint$fit$fit@stan_args[[1]]$max_depth
if (is.null(joint_max_depth)) joint_max_depth <- 10
joint_at_depth <- mean(np_joint$Value[np_joint$Parameter == "treedepth__"] >=
                         joint_max_depth)
w_ll5 <- vapply(level_weights, function(w) w[["ecxll5"]], numeric(1))
less_weight <- if (w_ll5[["Cu"]] < w_ll5[["Zn"]]) "copper than for zinc" else
  "zinc than for copper"
# Give one decimal place where rounding would print 0 or 100 per cent.
depth_pct <- 100 * joint_at_depth
depth_text <- sprintf(if (round(depth_pct) %in% c(0, 100) &&
                          !depth_pct %in% c(0, 100)) "%.1f" else "%.0f",
                      depth_pct)
```

A joint refit conditions on one equation per level, so the model uncertainty
that the per-level averages represent is absent from it. `ecxll5` was given only
part of the weight at each level, so the intervals of the joint refit omit the
uncertainty that the other equations represent (more for `r less_weight`). The
refit recorded
`r joint_divergent` divergent `r if (joint_divergent == 1) "transition" else "transitions"` in `r joint_draws` post-warmup draws,
with a largest R-hat of `r sprintf("%.3f", max(pars_joint$Rhat))` and a smallest
bulk effective sample size of `r round(min(pars_joint$Bulk_ESS))` over the
population-level, family and group-level SD parameters. Of its transitions,
`r depth_text` per cent reached the maximum tree depth of
`r joint_max_depth`, which Stan treats as a limit on the efficiency of the
sampler, not on the validity of the draws.

# Fitting the levels in parallel

`bnec_group()` has two loops that a `future` plan could drive: the levels, and
the equations within each level. The section *Fitting the model set in parallel*
of the [Multi model usage][e2] vignette describes how `bnec()` uses a plan for
the equations of one set, and it applies within a level.

A single plan drives only one of these loops, because `future` evaluates a
nested future sequentially unless the plan is itself a list. `bnec_group()`
therefore counts the rounds of one fit that each arrangement would take, uses
the arrangement with fewer rounds, and prints a message stating which it used.
With *L* levels, *M* equations and *W* workers, the levels take
`ceiling(L / W) * M` rounds and the equations `L * ceiling(M / W)`, and a tie is
resolved in favour of the equations. For example, seven levels of eleven
equations take 22 rounds with the levels in parallel against 21 with the
equations at four workers, but 11 against 14 at eight workers.

To drive both loops, give the plan as a list:

```{r parallel-group-nested, eval = FALSE}
library(future)
plan(list(tweak(multisession, workers = 2),
          tweak(multisession, workers = I(4))))
fit <- bnec_group(y ~ crf(x, model = "decline"), data = my_data,
                  group_var = "site", seed = 17)
plan(sequential)
```

The outer element drives the level loop and the inner element the equation loop,
so this example fits two levels at a time with four equations within each, on
eight workers. A nested plan is used as given, without counting. The `I()`
around the inner worker count is required: `future` sets `mc.cores` to 1 inside
a worker, `parallelly` reads that as the core budget and refuses a bare
`workers = 4`, and `I()` tells `parallelly` that the number is intended.
`plan(list(sequential, tweak(multisession, workers = I(8))))` fits the levels
one at a time instead, each over eight workers.

A nested plan of `multisession` strategies produces a warning from `future` once
per level (*added, removed, or modified connections*), because the inner cluster
keeps its connections open for reuse. No fit is affected, and
`options(future.connections.onMisuse = "ignore")` turns the warning off.

Fitting the levels in parallel also uses more memory, because each level worker
keeps its level's whole model-averaged set, and the message `bnec_group()`
prints gives the number of sets held at once. Levels fitted in parallel compile
identical Stan programs at the same time, so each worker process is given its
own `cmdstanr` compile directory (`bayesnec-stan-<host>-<pid>`, under
`cmdstanr_write_stan_file_dir` where that option is set), which can be deleted
once the workers finish. Under `rstan` with `rstan_options(auto_write = TRUE)`
the cache is one location shared by a forked worker and its parent, so we
recommend `multisession` for a parallel grouped call when that option is set
(see *The Stan backend* in [Multi model usage][e2]).

Each level is given its own seed, which is realised in the calling session
before the levels are dispatched, and derived from `seed` where one was passed.
That seed is passed to each equation fitted for the level, and is applied again
when the model weights and the model-averaging draw are computed. Both uses are
needed because sequential and parallel equation loops advance different random
number streams. A grouped call therefore gives identical estimates whichever
arrangement it used, so the number of workers on the machine does not change the
results. The realised seeds are stored on the returned object as `level_seeds`.

# Provenance

The fits shown were made when this vignette was precompiled, so the estimates
are those of the package version it ships with. The session that produced them
is recorded below.

```{r session}
packageVersion("bayesnec")
packageVersion("brms")
R.version.string
```

```{r adapt-delta, include = FALSE}
grouped_sets <- c(list(fit_ogl, fit_plate, fit_pgl, fit_pgl_cell), fits_tox$fits)
raised <- unique(unlist(lapply(grouped_sets, function(x) {
  Filter(function(m) {
    isTRUE(x$mod_fits[[m]]$fit$fit@stan_args[[1]]$control$adapt_delta == 0.99)
  }, x$success_models)
})))
```

No call names `iter`, `warmup` or `control`, so `bnec()`'s defaults apply: four
chains of 10000 iterations with 8000 of warmup, under `seed = 228`. `bnec()`
also raises `adapt_delta` from Stan's 0.8 to 0.99 for an equation whose mean a
group-level term could place outside the family's support. Of the equations
retained in the fits with a group-level term, that applied to `r eqs(raised)`,
and the others sampled at 0.8. @luter2025 sampled their analysis of these data
for longer, at 20000 iterations with 19000 of warmup and `adapt_delta = 0.99`.
The behaviour of nested `future` plans described under *Fitting the levels in
parallel* was measured on R 4.6.1 with `future` 1.70.0.

`lum31` was built by `data-raw/lum31.R` from the AIMS data repository deposit
that accompanies @luter2025. The source records are not distributed with the
package, and what was derived from them, and how, is described in `?lum31`.

